<design_notes>
A note on the testing philosophy here: I deliberately kept these tests at the contract level rather than pinning exact values. The contract of `coerce_row` is really three things — it returns a dict, it preserves the key set, and it doesn't blow up on arbitrary CSV-shaped input. Those are what regressions in practice look like: someone swaps the dict comprehension for a loop and drops a key, or a refactor starts mutating the caller's row. The tests catch all three.

Asserting `result["age"] == 30` buys you very little: if the coercion logic is wrong, that assertion just gets updated in the same commit as the bug, so it mostly creates churn on refactors. Type-level checks plus structural invariants give the same regression signal with far less brittleness. The mixed-row smoke test matters too, because `int()`/`float()`/`None` branches interact — a suite of per-type golden assertions tends to miss the interaction, while "runs to completion on a row containing every category" exercises all branches in one pass.

If you want property-style coverage later, hypothesis would fit this function well, since the invariants (idempotence on the input dict, key preservation) are exactly what property tests express cleanly.
</design_notes>

